WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.490
Yesterday a girl in my class asked a

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:06.170
chatbot how many legs a spider has.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.000
It said six, and it sounded very sure!

4
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.340
But a spider has eight legs.

5
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.800
So why would a clever

6
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.240
computer get it wrong?

7
00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.230
Today: why AI makes guesses, and

8
00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.210
how you can check them.

9
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.750
AI learns from lots and lots of examples.

10
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.380
A chatbot has read millions of sentences.

11
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.360
From them, it learns which

12
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.000
words often go together.

13
00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.350
Then it guesses an answer,

14
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:38.830
one word at a time.

15
00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.150
A guess can be a very good guess.

16
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.910
But sometimes it is wrong.

17
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.460
And a chatbot doesn't feel unsure, so it

18
00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.680
sounds just as sure when it's wrong.

19
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:54.450
Think of a classmate who

20
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.930
always answers fast.

21
00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:57.750
Usually they're right.

22
00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.570
But when they don't know, they answer

23
00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.870
just as fast and just as loud.

24
00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:04.590
Quick check!

25
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.430
A chatbot answers your question very

26
00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.790
fast, and it sounds very sure.

27
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.560
Does that mean it's right?

28
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.000
No, not always.

29
00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.710
A quick, sure answer can

30
00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:17.990
still be a wrong guess.

31
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.540
That's why we check.

32
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:21.500
How do you check?

33
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.100
Look in your school book.

34
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.100
Ask a teacher, or a grown-up at home.

35
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.670
And see if two good sources agree.

36
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.390
Some answers matter more than others.

37
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.140
A funny poem?

38
00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:36.190
Just enjoy it.

39
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.700
But your health, your safety and your

40
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.460
homework facts: always check.

41
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.860
In the app, a chatbot tells

42
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.180
you about the river Nile.

43
00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.430
Tap each part of its answer:

44
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.650
right, wrong, or check a book?

45
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.380
One part sounds sure, but it's made up!

46
00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:57.170
Your turn.

47
00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.080
A chatbot tells you a plant in the

48
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.580
school garden is safe to eat.

49
00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.130
What do you do?

50
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.330
Ask a grown-up first: your

51
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.510
safety matters most.

52
00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.250
Remember: AI learns from

53
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.250
examples and makes guesses.

54
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.250
A sure answer can still be wrong.

55
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.560
When it matters, check with

56
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:18.820
a book or a grown-up.

57
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.280
Try it tomorrow: play “Robot or right?”

58
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:24.590
with your family.

59
00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.120
Someone reads out facts in a very sure

60
00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.820
voice, some true and some made up.

61
00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.320
Everyone else says: true,

62
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.020
false, or let's check a book!
